{"step_index":134,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-18T07:24:13Z","input_tokens":3189,"cache_read_tokens":225324,"output_tokens":20055,"thinking":"Initial hypothesis: unlimited resources allow for the creation of a definitive Spider-Verse edition. The goal expands to include a full 2,161-frame 1080p master film and a V5-vs-V6 split-screen comparison. This is the new, more complete objective.\n\n\nThe initial plan is to build a robust and highly parameterized pipeline, starting with scripts for sound synthesis and muxing, capable of handling both short clips and the full film, including a split-screen comparison mode. Examining the input requirements for the sound synthesis script, it accesses both the base engine sound and the existing audio track, ensuring that the script can then produce the mixed audio for the V6 version of the project.\n\n\nThe soundtrack's reaction to on-screen events requires careful synchronization. Visual glitches and impacts necessitate corresponding audio manipulations, specifically short glitches and sub-bass drops timed precisely to match the visuals. This technique will significantly enhance the immersive effect, mimicking professional sound design.\n\n\nCareful synchronization of audio to visual events requires precise millisecond-level timestamps for critical events.  These include gear shifts, shot cuts, and specific visual impacts or glitches.  The goal is to design a sound pipeline that produces distinct, clear sonic layers across all universes, avoiding any muddiness, harshness, or clipping.\n\n\nThe sonic architecture of the sound synthesis pipeline is designed to create distinct layers, starting with a base telemetry engine sound for each universe. For Act I, a graphic comic effect is achieved through transient shaping, analog tape saturation, and rhythmic micro-pulse modulation synchronized to animation. Specifically, gearshifts and upshifts are emphasized with vinyl-like transient